            <ArticleTitle>Emergency treatment of headache.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>749-61</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Headache is a frequent presenting complaint in the emergency department. Once a diagnosis is established, and significant organic disease can be ruled out, relief of pain must be addressed. Referral for follow-up care and narcotic habituation is a recurrent problem for the emergency physician. This article discusses the differential diagnosis of headache, evaluation of the emergency room patient, and treatment of the patient with headache.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
